" (https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18.pdf)\nciteturn56view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 21; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.18.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1442\nL0@P0: Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), pages 279–299\nL1@P0: August 11-16, 2024 ©2024 Association for Computational Linguistics\nL2@P0: SCIMON : Scientific Inspiration Machines Optimized for Novelty\nL3@P0: Qingyun Wang1, Doug Downey2, Heng Ji1, Tom Hope2,3\nL4@P0: 1 University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign 2 Allen Institute for Artificial Intelligence (AI2)\nL5@P0: 3 The Hebrew University of Jerusalem\nL6@P0: {tomh,doug}@allenai.org, {qingyun4,hengji}@illinois.edu\nL7@P0: Abstract\nL8@P0: We explore and enhance the ability of neu\u0002ral language models to generate novel scien\u0002tific directions grounded in literature. Work\nL9@P0: on literature-based hypothesis generation has\nL10@P0: traditionally focused on binary link prediction—\nL11@P0: severely limiting the expressivity of hypothe\u0002ses. This line of work also does not focus on\nL12@P0: optimizing novelty. We take a dramatic depar\u0002ture with a novel setting in which models use\nL13@P0: as input background contexts (e.g., problems,\nL14@P0: experimental settings, goals), and output natu\u0002ral language ideas grounded in literature. We\nL15@P0: present SCIMON, a modeling framework that\nL16@P0: uses retrieval of “inspirations” from past scien\u0002tific papers, and explicitly optimizes for novelty\nL17@P0: by iteratively comparing to prior papers and up\u0002dating idea suggestions until sufficient novelty\nL18@P0: is achieved. Comprehensive evaluations reveal\nL19@P0: that GPT-4 tends to generate ideas with over\u0002all low technical depth and novelty, while our\nL20@P0: methods partially mitigate this issue. Our work\nL21@P0: represents a first step toward evaluating and\nL22@P0: developing language models that generate new\nL23@P0: ideas derived from the scientific literature1.\nL24@P0: 1 Introduction\nL25@P0: Can machines mine scientific papers and learn to\nL26@P0: suggest new directions? The idea that information\nL27@P0: from the literature can be used for automatically\nL28@P0: generating hypotheses has been around for decades\nL29@P0: (Swanson, 1986). To date, the focus has been on a\nL30@P0: specific setting: hypothesizing links between pairs\nL31@P0: of concepts (often in drug discovery applications\nL32@P0: (Henry and McInnes, 2017), e.g., new drug-disease\nL33@P0: links), where concepts are obtained from papers or\nL34@P0: knowledge bases previously derived from papers\nL35@P0: (Sybrandt et al., 2020; Nadkarni et al., 2021).\nL36@P0: This common setting has fundamental draw\u0002backs. Reducing the “language of scientific ideas”\nL37@P0: (Hope et al., 2023) to this simplistic form limits\nL38@P0: 1Code, data, and resources are publicly available for re\u0002search purposes: https://github.com/eaglew/clbd.\nL39@P0: Background\nL40@P0: Context\nL41@P0: Inspiration\nL42@P0: Retrieval\nL43@P0: Idea\nL44@P0: Generation\nL45@P0: Iterative\nL46@P0: Novelty\nL47@P0: Boosting\nL48@P0: Prior Literature\nL49@P0: {(Background_i, idea_i)}\nL50@P0: 𝛥\nL51@P0: Problems,\nL52@P0: motivations,\nL53@P0: focus points…\nL54@P0: “Given [context],\nL55@P0: a [new idea],\nL56@P0: 𝛥 vs. prior work…”\nL57@P0: Figure 1: SCIMON takes background context and gen\u0002erates ideas grounded in literature inspirations, optimiz\u0002ing novelty by iteratively comparing to related work.\nL58@P0: the expressivity of the hypotheses we can hope to\nL59@P0: generate, and does not capture nuanced contexts\nL60@P0: that scientists consider: target application settings,\nL61@P0: requirements and constraints, motivations and chal\u0002lenges. In light of the strong progress recently\nL62@P0: made with large language models (LLMs), in this\nL63@P0: paper we explore a dramatically different setting:\nL64@P0: models that take descriptions of problem contexts—\nL65@P0: and return natural language suggestions of novel\nL66@P0: scientific directions that are grounded in literature.\nL67@P0: We develop a framework named SCIMON (Sci\u0002entific Inspiration Machines with Optimization for\nL68@P0: Novelty), named after Nobel laureate and AI pi\u0002oneer Herbert Simon who authored early foun\u0002dational work on automated scientific discovery\nL69@P0: (Newell and Simon, 1956; Simon, 1973). We first\nL70@P0: present an automated data collection methodology\nL71@P0: that collects examples of past problems and pro\u0002posed ideas from scientific papers. We then use this\nL72@P0: data for both fine-tuning and in-context training\nL73@P0: of LLMs—training them to take problem descrip\u0002tions and output proposed ideas to address them.\nL74@P0: We observe that state-of-art LLMs (e.g., GPT-4\nL75@P0: (OpenAI, 2023)) struggle with generating novel\nL76@P0: scientific ideas, and contribute a new modeling\nL77@P0: framework for generating hypotheses that makes\nL78@P0: progress in improving the hypothesis generation\nL79@P0-1: 279ability of LLMs (Figure 1). Given a background\nL80@P1: problem description, models first dynamically re\u0002trieve inspirations from past literature in the form\nL81@P1: of related problems and their solutions along with\nL82@P1: contexts from a scientific knowledge graph. These\nL83@P1: retrieved inspirations serve to ground the generated\nL84@P1: ideas in existing literature. We then endow models\nL85@P1: with the ability to iteratively boost the novelty of\nL86@P1: generated ideas. Given an idea I generated by the\nL87@P1: LLM at step t, the model compares I with existing\nL88@P1: research in the literature; if it finds strongly overlap\u0002ping research, the model is tasked with updating its\nL89@P1: idea to be more novel relative to prior work (much\nL90@P1: like a good researcher would do). We also intro\u0002duce an in-context contrastive model which encour\u0002ages novelty with respect to background context.\nL91@P1: We perform the first comprehensive evaluation\nL92@P1: of language models for generating scientific ideas\nL93@P1: in our new generative, contextual setting. We fo\u0002cus on AI/NLP ideas to facilitate analysis by AI\nL94@P1: researchers themselves, and also demonstrate gen\u0002eralization to the biomedical domain. We design\nL95@P1: extensive evaluation experiments using human an\u0002notators with domain expertise to assess relevance,\nL96@P1: utility, novelty, and technical depth. Our methods\nL97@P1: substantially improve the ability of LLMs in our\nL98@P1: task; however, analyses show that ideas still fall far\nL99@P1: behind scientific papers in terms of novelty, depth\nL100@P1: and utility—raising fundamental challenges toward\nL101@P1: building models that generate scientific ideas.\nL102@P1: 2 Background and New Setting\nL103@P1: We begin with a brief description of related work\nL104@P1: and background. We then present our novel setting.\nL105@P1: Literature-based discovery Nearly four decades\nL106@P1: have passed since Don Swanson pioneered\nL107@P1: Literature-Based Discovery (LBD), based on the\nL108@P1: premise that the literature can be used for gen\u0002erating hypotheses (Swanson, 1986). LBD has\nL109@P1: been focused on a very specific, narrow type of\nL110@P1: hypothesis: links between pairs of concepts (often\nL111@P1: drugs/diseases). The classic formalization of LBD\nL112@P1: goes back to Swanson (1986) who proposed the\nL113@P1: “ABC” model where two concepts (terms) A and\nL114@P1: C are hypothesized as linked if they both co-occur\nL115@P1: with some intermediate concept B in papers. More\nL116@P1: recent work has used word vectors (Tshitoyan et al.,\nL117@P1: 2019) or link prediction models (Wang et al., 2019;\nL118@P1: Sybrandt et al., 2020; Xu et al., 2023) to discover\nL119@P1: scientific hypotheses as pairwise links between con\u0002cepts. A tightly related body of research focuses\nL120@P1: on scientific knowledge graph link prediction (Nad\u0002karni et al., 2021), where predicted links may cor\u0002respond to new hypotheses, and knowledge bases\nL121@P1: are reflections of existing scientific knowledge in\nL122@P1: specific domains, derived from literature. A fun\u0002damental gap in this line of work is in the lack of\nL123@P1: approaches for modeling nuanced contexts (Sosa\nL124@P1: and Altman, 2022) (e.g., the specific settings in\nL125@P1: which a drug may be relevant for a disease) for gen\u0002erating ideas in open-ended problem settings with\nL126@P1: unbounded hypothesis spaces, and for optimizing\nL127@P1: novelty. Our setting can be viewed as a radical\nL128@P1: departure addressing the limitations in existing set\u0002tings.\nL129@P1: LLMs for Scientific Innovation Large language\nL130@P1: models (LLMs) have made remarkable progress in\nL131@P1: interpreting and producing natural language con\u0002tent and handling knowledge-intensive tasks such\nL132@P1: as in the medical domain (Nori et al., 2023). Very\nL133@P1: recent work (Boiko et al., 2023) has explored the\nL134@P1: use of LLMs in a robotic chemistry lab setting,\nL135@P1: planning chemical syntheses of known compounds\nL136@P1: and executing experiments. Robotic lab settings\nL137@P1: are inherently limited to narrow sub-areas where\nL138@P1: such experiments are possible and relevant. Other\nL139@P1: very recent work (Huang et al., 2023) used LLMs\nL140@P1: to produce code for machine learning tasks such as\nL141@P1: Kaggle competitions, finding that a GPT-4 agent\nL142@P1: achieved 0% accuracy on research challenges such\nL143@P1: as BabyLM (Warstadt et al., 2023). GPT-4 has been\nL144@P1: anecdotally reported as having “strengths less like\nL145@P1: those of having a human co-author, and more like a\nL146@P1: mathematician working with a calculator” (Carlini,\nL147@P1: 2023). Our goal is to conduct a non-anecdotal eval\u0002uation and enhancement of strong LLMs’ ability to\nL148@P1: generate novel open-ended scientific ideas.\nL149@P1: 2.1 SCIMON Problem Setting\nL150@P1: We are motivated by imagining an AI-based assis\u0002tant that suggests ideas in natural language. The\nL151@P1: assistant takes as input background context B con\u0002sisting of (1) current problems, motivations, experi\u0002mental settings and constraints, denoted as M; and\nL152@P1: optionally (2) a seed term v that should be a focus\nL153@P1: point of the generated idea I. The seed term is mo\u0002tivated by considering a user-provided cue for the\nL154@P1: model to limit its hypothesis space. Importantly,\nL155@P1: generated ideas should not merely paraphrase the\nL156@P1: background—the output should be novel with re\u0002spect to B and the broader literature corpus. Fig\u0002ure 2 illustrates the setting, showing a background\nL157@P1-2: 2801. Continual learning (CL) aims to enable\nL158@P2: information systems to learn from a\nL159@P2: continuous data stream across time...\nL160@P2: ...a method that combines continual learning with\nL161@P2: a dynamic knowledge distillation approach for\nL162@P2: efficient knowledge acquisition ...\nL163@P2: 1. Different from previous knowledge distillation\nL164@P2: methods ... student model learns from teacher\nL165@P2: model for incremental knowledge extraction ...\nL166@P2: ...continual learning for knowledge acquisition...\nL167@P2: This approach is more efficient than exhaustive\nL168@P2: knowledge acquistion is done by pre-training on all existing data...\nL169@P2: using Method Current pre-trained...\nL170@P2: Initial Idea\nL171@P2: used-for\nL172@P2: used-for\nL173@P2: used-for\nL174@P2: linked knowledge graph inductive learning\nL175@P2: techniques\nL176@P2: perceptron\nL177@P2: learning\nL178@P2: cross - lingual\nL179@P2: transfer learning\nL180@P2: used-for\nL181@P2: knowledge acquistion\nL182@P2: collaborative web text\nL183@P2: annotation editor\nL184@P2: term clustering\nL185@P2: techniques image matching\nL186@P2: chinese open IE system\nL187@P2: ...\nL188@P2: ...\nL189@P2: episodic memory in\nL190@P2: lifelong language learning\nL191@P2: lamol: language\nL192@P2: modeling for ...\nL193@P2: megatron-lm: training\nL194@P2: multi-billion parameter...\nL195@P2: don't stop pretraining:\nL196@P2: adapt language ...\nL197@P2: ...\nL198@P2: ELLE: Efficient Lifelong Pre\u0002training for Emerging Data\nL199@P2: Inspiration Retrieval\nL200@P2: 1st Novelty Iteration\nL201@P2: Retrieved Similar Ideas\nL202@P2: Final Idea\nL203@P2: Problem/Motivation:\nL204@P2: ... streaming data of\nL205@P2: various sources may\nL206@P2: continuously grow ...\nL207@P2: requires plms to integrate\nL208@P2: the information from all\nL209@P2: the sources in a lifelong\nL210@P2: manner... pre-training on\nL211@P2: all existing data, such a\nL212@P2: process is expensive.\nL213@P2: Seed Term:\nL214@P2: knowledge acquisition\nL215@P2: Semantic\nL216@P2: Neighbors\nL217@P2: KG\nL218@P2: Neighbors\nL219@P2: Citation\nL220@P2: Neighbors\nL221@P2: Retrieval from Literature Papers\nL222@P2: Retrieval from Literature Papers\nL223@P2: Novelty Threshold Check\nL224@P2: Novelty Threshold Check\nL225@P2: Retrieved Similar Ideas\nL226@P2: Input:\nL227@P2: Background\nL228@P2: Context ... a method that leverages memory-augmented\nL229@P2: neural networks for knowledge acquisition in a\nL230@P2: lifelong learning scenario...\nL231@P2: Figure 2: Architecture overview. Our models retrieve inspirations and then pass the background input and retrieved\nL232@P2: inspirations to an LM-based generation module, which iteratively optimizes novelty. Input from Qin et al. (2022).\nL233@P2: Used-for\nL234@P2: Method\nL235@P2: (Target)\nL236@P2: Task\nL237@P2: (Seed)\nL238@P2: ... This requires plms to integrate the information from\nL239@P2: all the sources in a lifelong manner...\nL240@P2: Background\nL241@P2: Sentence\nL242@P2: Target\nL243@P2: Sentence\nL244@P2: ...function preserved model expansion ... improve the\nL245@P2: efficiency of knowledge acquisition ...\nL246@P2: Figure 3: We use IE to obtain literature data for our\nL247@P2: approach: problems/motivations (background) and pro\u0002posed ideas (target), as well as salient seed terms.\nL248@P2: text that describes problems with “pretrained lan\u0002guage models” in the lifelong integration of in\u0002formation sources, including computational costs.\nL249@P2: The assistant aims to generate an idea for perform\u0002ing “knowledge acquisition” within this context.\nL250@P2: Given this input, we aim to generate a full sentence\nL251@P2: describing a novel idea.\nL252@P2: 2.2 Automated Training Data Collection\nL253@P2: We obtain training data derived from papers with\nL254@P2: scientific information extraction (IE) models—\nL255@P2: extracting past examples of background sentences\nL256@P2: and corresponding ideas (e.g., descriptions of meth\u0002ods used for specific problems in the background\nL257@P2: sentences), along with salient entities as seed terms.\nL258@P2: This data is used for training in both in-context\nL259@P2: learning and fine-tuning setups.\nL260@P2: We construct a corpus D from 67,408 ACL An\u0002thology papers from S2ORC (Lo et al., 2020) (we\nL261@P2: later also conduct an experiment with a biomedical\nL262@P2: corpus §4.1). Given a title and the correspond\u0002ing abstract from a document d, to select prob\u0002lem/motivation sentences M we first perform sci\u0002entific sentence classification (Cohan et al., 2019)\nL263@P2: to classify sentences from the abstract into one of\nL264@P2: {Background, Method, Objective}, selecting sen\u0002tences with labels of Background and treating the\nL265@P2: remaining sentences as target sentences T which\nL266@P2: will serve as desired output examples (Figure 3).\nL267@P2: For seed term selection, we apply a state-of\u0002the-art scientific IE system (Ye et al., 2022) to T\nL268@P2: to extract entities corresponding to Task, Method,\nL269@P2: Evaluation Metric, Material, and relations of\nL270@P2: the form [method,used-for,task]—mentions\nL271@P2: of methods and the tasks they are used for, ma\u0002terials used for tasks, etc. We treat the head (e.g.,\nL272@P2: method) or tail (e.g., task) entity as the seed term,\nL273@P2: and name the other entity (tail/head, respectively)\nL274@P2: as a target term t ∈ T . Continuing our example\nL275@P2: from Figure 2, Figure 3 shows how the seed and tar\u0002get terms (“knowledge acquisition” and “function\nL276@P2: preserved model expansion”) are extracted from T .\nL277@P2: During training, each instance contains (B,T ) pairs;\nL278@P2: during evaluation, target information is removed.\nL279@P2: We use SciCo (Cattan et al., 2021) to obtain\nL280@P2: coreference links for entity normalization, and use\nL281@P2: ScispaCy (Neumann et al., 2019) to replace ab\u0002breviations with a more informative long form.\nL282@P2: We also collect paper metadata, including the ci\u0002tation network Gc. We split our dataset tempo\u0002rally (train/dev/test correspond to papers from years\nL283@P2: <2021 / 2021 / 2022 respectively). For our experi\u0002ments, we used model checkpoints trained on data\nL284@P2: preceding 2022, avoiding the risk of data contami\u0002nation (§6). Table 1 shows data statistics.2\nL285@P2: 2More details are in Appendix C.\nL286@P2-3: 281Quality of IE Preprocessing During preprocess\u0002ing, we only keep high-confidence outputs from IE\nL287@P3: models to reduce errors. We observe this removes\nL288@P3: many of the noisy cases. To validate this, we man\u0002ually evaluate the precision of each preprocessing\nL289@P3: step on a random sample of papers and observe\nL290@P3: that all steps yield high precision (91%-100%) ex\u0002cept relation extraction (65%); in total, the rate of\nL291@P3: instances passing all steps was 79.7%.3\nL292@P3: Gold Test Set We create a high-quality, clean test\nL293@P3: set. We remove test instances where models can\nL294@P3: trivially use surface-level background information\nL295@P3: to infer the ground truth to create a more chal\u0002lenging set, selecting instances with low similarity\nL296@P3: between background and ground truth sentences.\nL297@P3: We compute the cosine similarity between each\nL298@P3: instance’s background and corresponding ground\nL299@P3: truth sentence in the test set and take pairs with\nL300@P3: similarity ≤ 0.074, which amounts to the tenth per\u0002centile of pairs. We further annotate this subset\nL301@P3: to create a gold subset. We manually exclude in\u0002stances with trivial overlap between ground truth\nL302@P3: and background, remove cases with irrelevant back\u0002ground, and retain only instances where the tar\u0002get relation (from which the seed term is taken)\nL303@P3: is salient to the target sentence. We also remove\nL304@P3: test pairs that have unexplained terms in the back\u0002ground. We obtain a total of 194 instances.4\nL305@P3: Split Forward Backward Total\nL306@P3: Train 55,884 58,426 114,310\nL307@P3: Valid 7,938 8,257 16,195\nL308@P3: Test 2,623 2,686 5,309\nL309@P3: Table 1: Dataset statistics. Considering a relation of the\nL310@P3: form [v used-for u], we define [v used-for ?] as\nL311@P3: forward, and [? used-for u] as backward.\nL312@P3: 3 SCIMON Models\nL313@P3: We present a new module to retrieve inspirations\nL314@P3: as contextual input (§3.1). Then, we describe\nL315@P3: another module to generate ideas given the con\u0002text+inspiration (§3.2). Finally, we introduce a\nL316@P3: new iterative novelty optimization method to fur\u0002ther improve idea quality (§3.3).5\nL317@P3: 3\nL318@P3: See Table 6 in Appendix.\nL319@P3: 4\nL320@P3: Full annotation details are in Appendix C.\nL321@P3: 5Training and hyperparameter details in Appendix B.\nL322@P3: 3.1 Inspiration Retrieval Module\nL323@P3: We take broad inspiration from cognitive aspects of\nL324@P3: innovation (Hope et al., 2023): when researchers\nL325@P3: generate a new idea, they are grounded in a web\nL326@P3: of existing concepts and papers bearing on the new\nL327@P3: idea. We aim to enrich the context of each back\u0002ground by retrieving “inspirations”— pieces of in\u0002formation that can guide hypothesis generation. As\nL328@P3: illustrated in Figure 2, for a given instance of the\nL329@P3: SCIMON task, our retrieval augmentation can re\u0002trieve from three types of sources. Each source\nL330@P3: uses a different form of query and output.\nL331@P3: Semantic Neighbors For a given prob\u0002lem/motivation as input, ideas proposed for related\nL332@P3: problems in the training set can serve as a guiding\nL333@P3: reference for generating a new idea. Given the\nL334@P3: background context B with a seed term v and\nL335@P3: problem/motivation M, we construct a base\nL336@P3: input b: a concatenation of M with a prompt P\nL337@P3: belonging to one of two templates: “v is used\nL338@P3: for p” or “v is done by using p”, where p is one\nL339@P3: of Task/Method/Material/Metric. In short, b :=\nL340@P3: P ⊕ context:M. For example, in Figure 2, the\nL341@P3: concatenation is “Knowledge acquisition is done\nL342@P3: by using Method; Context:...requires plms to\nL343@P3: integrate information...lifelong manner...”.\nL344@P3: We then retrieve inputs from the training set that\nL345@P3: are semantically related to a new base input b, and\nL346@P3: obtain target sentences T corresponding to each\nL347@P3: retrieved training input. We extract the target term\nL348@P3: t ∈ T matching the seed term in b (§2.2) as in\u0002spiration for input b. Simply put, this means we\nL349@P3: use as inspiration the salient aspect of the solution\nL350@P3: proposed in T , which we found empirically to help\nL351@P3: remove noisy/irrelevant information in T . For ex\u0002ample, in Figure 2, we find “informative entities\nL352@P3: are done by using Method context: in this work, we\nL353@P3: aim at equipping pre-trained language models with\nL354@P3: structured knowledge.” as similar to the input and\nL355@P3: use t =“linked knowledge graph” as inspiration.\nL356@P3: Technically, we first construct a fully connected\nL357@P3: graph GS based on the training set where each node\nL358@P3: is a pair of input text bi and target term ti. We\nL359@P3: define the weight between two nodes i and j as\nL360@P3: the cosine similarity between bi and bj based on\nL361@P3: representations from SentenceBERT (Reimers and\nL362@P3: Gurevych, 2019) (all-mpnet-base-v2). Given b,\nL363@P3: we first insert it into GS and compute the weights\nL364@P3: of its connected edges. We then retrieve neighbors\nL365@P3: input text {b1, . . . , bk} from the training set with\nL366@P3: the largest edge weight, where k is the number of\nL367@P3-4: 282retrieved instances. We consider the corresponding\nL368@P4: target terms {t1, . . . , tk} as semantic inspirations.\nL369@P4: KG Neighbors We also explore enriching the\nL370@P4: context by linking it to a background KG with in\u0002formation on related methods and tasks. Using the\nL371@P4: same IE process used to extract our training exam\u0002ples (§2.2), we create a global background KG GB\nL372@P4: which covers all papers in the corpus DY prior to a\nL373@P4: given year Y (i.e., the nodes in GB correspond to\nL374@P4: tasks/methods/materials/metrics, and the edges are\nL375@P4: used-for relations, extracted and normalized from\nL376@P4: across the entire corpus as described earlier). Then,\nL377@P4: given a seed term v at query time, we select ad\u0002jacent nodes {n1, n2, ...} from GB as inspirations.\nL378@P4: As an example, in Figure 2, the neighbor nodes of\nL379@P4: “knowledge acquisition” include “collaborative web\nL380@P4: text annotation editor”, “image matching”, etc.,\nL381@P4: which we select as inspirations.\nL382@P4: Citation Neighbors Another notion of contex\u0002tual relatedness we explore is via citation graph\nL383@P4: links. Here, given as input background context B,\nL384@P4: we assume access to the original source document\nL385@P4: d from which B was extracted, and consider its\nL386@P4: cited paper title set Cd as potential candidates. This\nL387@P4: can be seen as a stronger assumption on informa\u0002tion available to the model— assuming a researcher\nL388@P4: using the model provides relevant candidate docu\u0002ments from which ideas could be pooled. Because\nL389@P4: the training set only contains papers before year\nL390@P4: Y, we only select papers CdY ⊆ Cd prior to year Y.\nL391@P4: We then retrieve the top-k titles with the highest\nL392@P4: cosine similarity to d from CdY based on their Sen\u0002tenceBERT embeddings as earlier. For instance,\nL393@P4: in Figure 2, the paper ELLE (Qin et al., 2022)\nL394@P4: cites the paper (de Masson d'Autume et al., 2019).\nL395@P4: Therefore, we choose the title “episodic memory\nL396@P4: in lifelong language learning” as inspiration infor\u0002mation.\nL397@P4: 3.2 Generation Module\nL398@P4: The idea generation module is given retrieved inspi\u0002rations i1, . . . , ik along with context M as input.\nL399@P4: In-Context Learning We experiment\nL400@P4: with recent state-of-the-art LLMs, GPT3.5\nL401@P4: davinci-003 (Ouyang et al., 2022) and GPT4\nL402@P4: gpt-4-0314 checkpoint (OpenAI, 2023). We first\nL403@P4: ask the model to generate sentences based on the\nL404@P4: seed term and the context in the zero-shot setting\nL405@P4: without any in-context examples (GPT3.5ZS,\nL406@P4: GPT4ZS). We then ask the model to generate\nL407@P4: sentences in a few-shot setting by prompting\nL408@P4: randomly chosen pairs of input and output from the\nL409@P4: training set (GPT3.5FS, GPT4FS). Inspired by Liu\nL410@P4: et al. (2022), we further employ a few-shot setting\nL411@P4: using semantically similar examples. Instead of\nL412@P4: random in-context examples, we use the top-k\nL413@P4: examples from the training set with the highest\nL414@P4: cosine similarity to the query (GPT3.5Retr). This\nL415@P4: few-shot retrieval setting differs from the semantic\nL416@P4: neighbor discussed above, in that we provide both\nL417@P4: the input and output of each instance rather than\nL418@P4: solely supplying target entities as additional input.\nL419@P4: Fine Tuning We fine-tune T5 (Raffel et al., 2020)\nL420@P4: (more recent models may be used too; see our\nL421@P4: biomedical experiment §4.1 fine-tuning an LLM).\nL422@P4: We observe that the generation models tend to copy\nL423@P4: phrases from the background context. For example,\nL424@P4: given the context “...hierarchical tables challenge\nL425@P4: numerical reasoning ...”, the model will generate\nL426@P4: “hierarchical table reasoning for question answering”\nL427@P4: as the top prediction. For generating suggestions of\nL428@P4: novel ideas, we wish to discourage overly copying\nL429@P4: from the background context. We introduce a new\nL430@P4: in-context contrastive objective, where negative ex\u0002amples are taken from the text in the input (e.g.,\nL431@P4: in Figure 2, the in-context negatives are plms, pre\u0002training, etc). We compute an InfoNCE loss (Oord\nL432@P4: et al., 2018) over the hidden states of the decoder,\nL433@P4: aiming to maximize the probability of the ground\nL434@P4: truth against those of in-context negatives:\nL435@P4: y\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn56view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41524-025-01540-6\",\"pattern\":\"precision\"}); Total lines: 1\nNo matching text found for \"precision\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn56view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41524-025-01540-6\",\"pattern\":\"expert\"}); Total lines: 1\nNo matching text found for \"expert\"---------